WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.115
How fast is ShellSort?

00:00:02.115 --> 00:00:05.251
It depends on the increment sequence

00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:07.726
and finding the best sequence

00:00:07.809 --> 00:00:09.871
is still an open problem.

00:00:09.954 --> 00:00:12.594
One pass sorts h subsequences of

00:00:12.677 --> 00:00:15.070
about n/h elements each

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:18.700
so it costs at most about n²/h.

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:21.743
Why are the early passes worth it?

00:00:21.743 --> 00:00:24.516
Because an array that is h-sorted

00:00:24.516 --> 00:00:27.288
and then k-sorted, stays h-sorted.

00:00:27.367 --> 00:00:29.743
The work of the earlier passes

00:00:29.823 --> 00:00:31.090
is never undone.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.300
With Shell's own increments, n/2

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:36.016
n/4, and so on

00:00:36.016 --> 00:00:38.090
the worst case is n².

00:00:38.161 --> 00:00:40.449
Hibbard's and Knuth's increments

00:00:40.521 --> 00:00:43.667
give n^(3/2), and Sedgewick's

00:00:43.667 --> 00:00:45.240
n^(4/3).

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:48.073
The average case is not known

00:00:48.158 --> 00:00:49.785
for most sequences.

00:00:49.871 --> 00:00:52.354
Experiments give roughly

00:00:52.439 --> 00:00:54.408
n^1.25

00:00:54.408 --> 00:00:57.148
up to n^1.3

00:00:57.148 --> 00:01:00.230
an empirical figure, not a theorem.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:04.369
And the best case, an already sorted input

00:01:04.369 --> 00:01:06.396
still runs every pass

00:01:06.396 --> 00:01:10.185
and each pass looks at every element once.

00:01:10.185 --> 00:01:12.740
That is n log n, not linear.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.477
Why can Shell's increments take n²?

00:01:16.477 --> 00:01:19.784
Here is the bad input for n = 8

00:01:19.859 --> 00:01:21.964
the four largest numbers sit

00:01:22.039 --> 00:01:23.618
in the odd positions

00:01:23.618 --> 00:01:27.000
and the four smallest in the even positions.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:29.168
The first gap is 4

00:01:29.168 --> 00:01:32.391
positions 0 and 4, 1 and 5

00:01:32.391 --> 00:01:33.301
and so on.

00:01:33.301 --> 00:01:36.606
Three shifts, and the large numbers are

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:38.590
still in odd positions.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.751
The next gap is 2

00:01:40.751 --> 00:01:44.462
the even positions form one subsequence

00:01:44.462 --> 00:01:47.449
and the odd positions the other.

00:01:47.449 --> 00:01:49.532
Six shifts, and still

00:01:49.532 --> 00:01:52.338
all the large numbers are odd.

00:01:52.428 --> 00:01:55.144
An even gap only ever compares

00:01:55.235 --> 00:01:57.860
positions of the same parity.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:01.738
So everything is left for the last pass.

00:02:01.738 --> 00:02:03.501
2 must pass 9.

00:02:03.501 --> 00:02:06.147
3 must pass 9 and 10.

00:02:06.147 --> 00:02:09.410
4 must pass 9, 10 and 11.

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:11.989
1 + 2 + 3

00:02:11.989 --> 00:02:15.240
6 shifts in the last pass alone.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:16.779
In general,

00:02:16.866 --> 00:02:20.179
the small number in position 2t

00:02:20.266 --> 00:02:23.492
has t large numbers in front of it.

00:02:23.579 --> 00:02:25.933
So the last pass makes

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:27.589
0 + 1 + 2

00:02:27.589 --> 00:02:30.641
up to n/2 - 1 shifts,

00:02:30.641 --> 00:02:32.820
that is O(n²).

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:35.594
The program in the notes builds

00:02:35.672 --> 00:02:37.548
this input for larger n.

00:02:37.626 --> 00:02:39.737
The last pass makes exactly

00:02:39.815 --> 00:02:41.145
that many shifts,

00:02:41.223 --> 00:02:43.803
and the count grows 16 times

00:02:43.881 --> 00:02:46.148
each time n grows 4 times.

00:02:46.148 --> 00:02:49.588
Knuth's increments, which include odd gaps,

00:02:49.588 --> 00:02:53.106
mix the two halves early: on the same input,

00:02:53.185 --> 00:02:56.390
n equal to 4096 costs

00:02:56.468 --> 00:02:59.048
about 32000 shifts

00:02:59.048 --> 00:03:00.690
against 2 million.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:02.266
On random input,

00:03:02.342 --> 00:03:04.641
both ShellSorts beat insertion

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:06.862
sort by orders of magnitude.

00:03:06.862 --> 00:03:09.314
For 100000 numbers,

00:03:09.314 --> 00:03:12.148
about 2.9 million shifts

00:03:12.148 --> 00:03:14.600
against 2.5 billion.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.886
Shell's own increments do

00:03:16.964 --> 00:03:18.513
well on random data.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:20.604
Their n² worst case

00:03:20.682 --> 00:03:23.470
needs special inputs, like this one.

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:25.827
Is ShellSort stable?

00:03:25.916 --> 00:03:28.404
Stable means that equal keys

00:03:28.493 --> 00:03:30.803
keep their original order.

00:03:30.803 --> 00:03:33.203
Take 3, 2, 2, 1

00:03:33.203 --> 00:03:36.046
and label the two 2s a and b

00:03:36.046 --> 00:03:38.090
so we can follow them.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:39.799
With gap 2,

00:03:39.799 --> 00:03:44.503
positions 0 and 2 hold 3 and 2b.

00:03:44.503 --> 00:03:48.058
Sorted, 2b jumps to the front

00:03:48.058 --> 00:03:49.312
over 2a.

00:03:49.312 --> 00:03:53.494
Positions 1 and 3, 2a and 1

00:03:53.494 --> 00:03:55.480
become 1, 2a.

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:59.296
The final insertion sort gives 1, 2b,

00:03:59.296 --> 00:04:00.451
2a, 3.

00:04:00.451 --> 00:04:02.679
The two 2s have swapped.

00:04:02.679 --> 00:04:05.319
Insertion sort alone is stable,

00:04:05.319 --> 00:04:07.134
but ShellSort is not.

00:04:07.217 --> 00:04:09.692
A long jump can carry one copy

00:04:09.775 --> 00:04:11.590
of a key past another.

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:15.208
Now a sort that compares nothing.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:18.246
Assume every value is an integer

00:04:18.338 --> 00:04:20.640
from 0 to m - 1

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.046
for a known m that is not too large.

00:04:24.046 --> 00:04:25.519
Here m is 5.

00:04:25.519 --> 00:04:28.373
Start with 5 empty buckets

00:04:28.373 --> 00:04:30.490
numbered 0 to 4.

00:04:30.840 --> 00:04:34.003
Put each element into the bucket whose

00:04:34.086 --> 00:04:36.500
number is the element itself.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:39.080
The bucket index is the value.

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:47.111
4, 2, 1, 2: 0, 3, 2, 1

00:04:47.111 --> 00:04:52.470
4, 0, 2, 3, 0.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.670
All the copies of a value

00:04:54.748 --> 00:04:56.621
land in the same bucket

00:04:56.699 --> 00:04:59.351
so a bucket only needs to remember

00:04:59.429 --> 00:05:00.833
how many it holds.

00:05:00.833 --> 00:05:03.953
That is why it is called counting sort

00:05:03.953 --> 00:05:06.606
3 zeros, 2 ones, 4 twos

00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:08.400
2 threes, 2 fours.

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:12.160
Then write the buckets back in order

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.293
3 zeros, 2 ones, 4 twos

00:05:15.293 --> 00:05:17.781
2 threes, and 2 fours.

00:05:17.781 --> 00:05:21.190
Sorted, without a single comparison.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:24.813
Here is the slides' pseudocode in Java.

00:05:24.813 --> 00:05:28.089
The array b of m counters starts at 0.

00:05:28.089 --> 00:05:31.210
The first loop walks through the input

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:34.318
and adds 1 to b[s[i]]

00:05:34.318 --> 00:05:38.160
the counter whose number is the value.

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:41.654
Then, for each bucket j in order

00:05:41.749 --> 00:05:44.131
the inner loop writes the

00:05:44.226 --> 00:05:47.466
value j back into s, b[j] times

00:05:47.466 --> 00:05:48.990
moving i along.

00:05:49.340 --> 00:05:51.001
What does it cost?

00:05:51.001 --> 00:05:54.415
Creating the counters costs O(m)

00:05:54.415 --> 00:05:56.998
and counting costs O(n).

00:05:56.998 --> 00:05:58.935
In the output loops

00:05:58.935 --> 00:06:02.534
the inner loop runs b[0] + b[1]

00:06:02.534 --> 00:06:05.855
and so on, which is n times in all

00:06:05.855 --> 00:06:08.900
and the outer loop runs m times.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:12.751
So the total is O(m + n) time

00:06:12.751 --> 00:06:15.280
with O(m) extra space.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:19.170
When m is O(n), that is linear time.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:20.671
Your turn.

00:06:20.671 --> 00:06:24.447
The exercise on the slides: 4, 3

00:06:24.447 --> 00:06:28.407
2, 1, 5, 2, 4, 1, 2, 0

00:06:28.407 --> 00:06:29.973
4, 2, 0.

00:06:29.973 --> 00:06:32.460
The largest value is 5

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.670
so we need 6 buckets.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:36.863
What are the 6 counts?

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:40.012
And what goes wrong if you call countingSort

00:06:40.089 --> 00:06:42.086
with m equal to 5?

00:06:42.086 --> 00:06:44.620
Pause the video and work it out.

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:52.260
The counts are 2, 2, 4, 1, 3

00:06:52.260 --> 00:06:52.743
1.

00:06:52.743 --> 00:06:55.160
They add up to 13

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:57.480
the number of elements.

00:06:57.830 --> 00:07:01.336
Written back: 0, 0, 1, 1

00:07:01.336 --> 00:07:05.442
four 2s, 3, three 4s, and 5.

00:07:05.542 --> 00:07:08.147
That is the second line of

00:07:08.247 --> 00:07:10.350
the example's output.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.103
With m equal to 5, there is no

00:07:13.178 --> 00:07:15.206
counter for the value 5.

00:07:15.281 --> 00:07:17.158
The line b[s[i]]++

00:07:17.233 --> 00:07:18.960
reaches b[5]

00:07:19.035 --> 00:07:21.288
and Java throws an

00:07:21.363 --> 00:07:23.165
ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException.

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:25.418
Always check that m is larger

00:07:25.493 --> 00:07:27.220
than the largest value.

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:30.355
The slides ask three questions.

00:07:30.355 --> 00:07:33.074
What if the range is very large?

00:07:33.156 --> 00:07:35.464
CountingSort spends time and

00:07:35.546 --> 00:07:37.524
space on all m counters,

00:07:37.524 --> 00:07:39.254
even the empty ones.

00:07:39.336 --> 00:07:42.385
Sorting 10 numbers below 1 billion

00:07:42.468 --> 00:07:44.940
would need a billion counters.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:48.401
Use it only when m is O(n); otherwise,

00:07:48.401 --> 00:07:51.120
radix sort or a comparison sort.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:54.379
How do we handle negative values?

00:07:54.379 --> 00:07:57.023
Shift them: find the minimum

00:07:57.111 --> 00:07:59.755
and put each value x in bucket

00:07:59.843 --> 00:08:01.606
x minus the minimum.

00:08:01.694 --> 00:08:04.074
The method countingSortRange

00:08:04.162 --> 00:08:06.189
in the notes does this.

00:08:06.189 --> 00:08:10.155
Minus 9 to 7 needs 17 buckets

00:08:10.155 --> 00:08:13.769
and the output is -9, -2,

00:08:13.769 --> 00:08:17.030
-2, 0, 3, 5, 7.

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:18.920
And the limitations:

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:20.999
the keys must be integers

00:08:20.999 --> 00:08:23.155
in a known and small range.

00:08:23.232 --> 00:08:25.002
And this simple version

00:08:25.079 --> 00:08:26.773
writes back bare keys.

00:08:26.773 --> 00:08:28.775
To sort records by a key,

00:08:28.775 --> 00:08:30.700
we need another version.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:32.094
To sum up.

00:08:32.094 --> 00:08:32.501
1:

00:08:32.501 --> 00:08:36.163
ShellSort's speed depends on its increments.

00:08:36.163 --> 00:08:39.662
Shell's own increments can take n²,

00:08:39.743 --> 00:08:41.615
Knuth's increments take

00:08:41.697 --> 00:08:43.487
n^(3/2).

00:08:43.487 --> 00:08:46.010
2: ShellSort works in place,

00:08:46.010 --> 00:08:47.800
but it is not stable.

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:51.656
3: counting sort uses the value

00:08:51.756 --> 00:08:53.859
as the bucket number

00:08:53.959 --> 00:08:57.465
and sorts n integers from 0 to m - 1

00:08:57.565 --> 00:09:00.870
in O(m + n) time.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:02.713
Try this yourself

00:09:02.713 --> 00:09:05.119
write a method sortLetters

00:09:05.202 --> 00:09:08.188
that sorts the lower-case letters of

00:09:08.271 --> 00:09:11.091
a string with twenty-six counters.

00:09:11.091 --> 00:09:13.164
"shellsort", as one word,

00:09:13.164 --> 00:09:16.731
should come out as e, h, l, l, o, r, s, s

00:09:16.731 --> 00:09:17.231
t.

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:20.491
In part three: a stable counting sort that

00:09:20.566 --> 00:09:22.071
moves whole records

00:09:22.071 --> 00:09:24.329
bucket sort for real numbers

00:09:24.329 --> 00:09:26.210
and radix sort, by hand.

